{"id": "50:16", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "de sanguínibus", "portuguese": "do sangue", "problem": "Latin plural (sanguines, 'bloods', i.e. bloodguilt) reduced to singular; the number change is allowed only for need, and the plural is sayable.", "fix": "Livrai-me dos sangues"}, {"latin": "exsultábit lingua mea justítiam tuam", "portuguese": "a minha língua exultará na vossa justiça", "problem": "justitiam is an accusative object: the tongue joyfully proclaims/celebrates God's justice. 'na vossa justiça' turns the object into the place or ground of rejoicing, changing the construction's sense.", "fix": "a minha língua exultará a vossa justiça"}]},
{"id": "50:20", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "Benígne fac ... Sion", "portuguese": "Sede benigno ... com Sião", "problem": "benigne fac is an action (deal kindly, do good to Sion), not a state; 'sede benigno' turns the imperative of doing into one of being.", "fix": "Tratai benignamente, Senhor, na vossa boa vontade, a Sião"}]}
], "overall": "A close, faithful rendering of the Miserere; tenses (future in 9, 10, 17, 18, 19, 21), moods and the Latin's key images are preserved, and every verse carries the same single mediant as the Latin with no flex or second mediant missing or added. The only faults are small: a lost passive in 9, the plural 'sanguinibus' and the accusative object of 'exsultabit' in 16, and 'benigne fac' rendered as a state rather than an act in 20."}
